{:edb/db-before-t 12445 :edb/db-after-t 12446 :edb/tx-data [[13194139545758 50 #inst "2026-11-03T19:27:34.236Z" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186539387 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186539387 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\right]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186539398 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+25t-20t=5(12+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12.5-\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q=12.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(12+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{4}\\cdot 12.5 \\\\ (12+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(12+t)^{3}Q & =12.5(12+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4}) & =12.5(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 12.5(12+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{4} & =12.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(12+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 2.5(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{(12)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{4}} & =-20 \\\\ C & =-20(12)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)-\\frac{20(12)^{4}}{(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =2.5(12+t)-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+t}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 60+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 60+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =2.5(12+24)-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+24}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =90-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{36}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈89.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186539398 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(25\\,\\frac{\\ca