kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\\\\n&=\\frac{36Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/min}=\\frac{3Q}{100-t}\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}&=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}\\\\&=0.96-\\frac{3Q}{100-t}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThus,\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100-t}Q=0.96.$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n$$\\begin{aligned}I(t)&=e^{\\int 3/(100-t)\\,dt}\\\\&=e^{-3\\ln|100-t|}\\\\&=e^{\\ln (100-t)^{-3}}\\\\&=(100-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nMultiplying both sides by $I(t)=(100-t)^{-3}$ yields\n$$\\begin{aligned}(100-t)^{-3}\\cdot(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100-t}Q)&=(100-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.96\\\\\n(100-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100-t)^{-4}Q&=0.96(100-t)^{-3}\\\\\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-3})&=0.96(100-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-3})\\,dt&=\\int 0.96(100-t)^{-3}\\,dt\\\\Q(100-t)^{-3}&=0.96\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}(100-t)^{-2}+C\\\\Q(t)&=0.48(100-t)+C(100-t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\nThe initial condition is $Q(0)=22.$ Thus,\n$$\\begin{aligned}0.48(100-0)+C(100-0)^{3}&=22\\\\\n48+C(100)^{3}&=22\\\\C(100)^{3}&=-26\\\\C&=- \\frac{13}{500000}.\\end{aligned}$$\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n$$Q(t)=- \\frac{12 t}{25} - \\frac{13 \\left(100 - t\\right)^{3}}{500000} + 48.$$\nWriting this in the supplied normalized form gives\n$$Q(t)=48(1-\\frac{t}{100})-26(1-\\frac{t}{100})^{3}.$$\nFinally, we have $\\alpha=48,\\beta=-26,$ and\n$$\\alpha-\\beta=48-(-26)=74.$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186540391 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of dissolved substance in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. The amount entering minus the amount leaving gives\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}.$$\nThe units of these rates are $\\text{kg/m