termine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d1/d19b70836b7b377c498fa505868563115706b588b0a2216d09c6ec738f746bbd.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $$\n\\displaystyle y=g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\left(\\left[-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x\\right]-\\left[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x\\right]\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left[\\left(-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}\\right)-0\\right] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left(-\\frac{32}{5}+8\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541081 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541081 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541084 1071 "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=16$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=6.$ Find $\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y].$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541084 1071 "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=16$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=6.$ Find $\\text{Var}\\left[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y\\right].$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541084 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y]={\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 16+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 6=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541084 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}\\left[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y\\right]={\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 16+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 6=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541087 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541087 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right].\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541088 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{9}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+5)(2x+k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{5+k}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5+k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5+k}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 5+k & =9 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541088 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{9}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+5)(2x+k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{5+k}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5+k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5+k}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 5+k & =9 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541090 1074 "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(0,\\pi )\\times ((-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\cup (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =((0,\\pi )\\times (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0))\\cup ((0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541090 1074 "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(0,\\pi )\\times \\left(\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0\\right)\\cup \\left(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left((0,\\pi )\\times \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0\\right)\\right)\\cup \\left((0,\\pi )\\times \\left(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541094 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V