ligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q\\right) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}(18-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 18\\,\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)-2.2{\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =7.2\\,\\left(1-\\frac{t}{18}\\right)-2.2{\\left(1-\\frac{t}{18}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =7.2,\\beta =-2.2,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =7.2-(-2.2)=9.4.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541126 1071 "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 1)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186541126 1071 "If $X∼B\\left(5,\\frac{2}{5}\\right),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 1)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186541126 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}