me{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 1)}{(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/1018201037350f6bd231a384219d03c03fc1e2165a088e158c9ba2b2bfbb1022.png)" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186542115 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\