,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{X|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}+{6}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{9}]-{\\left(\\frac{22}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+45-\\frac{484}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186542174 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ 