 outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\n![](images/48/48df22796616a48f9e9ed56474ced12392905e976fdec064b401894f30cbf49b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(7-y)-(-3)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y}.$\n\n![](images/f3/f3240bb3d94231ae5b786b01b2fe11f9e0ea7971978e54f26a4e36b84af71a28.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\,\\left(\\frac{6}{y}\\right)-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186545796 1074 "Let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ed/edea788893771f13f3afc7d9f49e8f1dead4e9b589ca78ef160b9bddccf867c5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=7-