iable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.5)=50\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.5)(1-0.5)=25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(50,25).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(43<X<53).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)=P(44\\le X\\le 52)≈P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5) & =P(\\frac{43.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{52.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.30\\le Z\\le 0.50) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z<-1.30) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z\\le -1.30) \\\\  & =Φ(0.50)-Φ(-1.30) \\\\  & =0.6915-0.0968 \\\\  & =0.5947\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)≈0.595.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.5947.$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186547076 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.5)=50\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.5)(1-0.5)=25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(50,25).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(43<X<53).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)=P(44\\le X\\le 52)≈P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5) & =P\\left(\\frac