n in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+15t-10t=(450+5t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\cdot {90}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\