l solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{15}{2}-7I-12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=2$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{15}{2}-7I(0)-12q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}-7\\cdot 2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=2$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{-0}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-4B{e}^{-4t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{19}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{19}{2} & =-3A{e}^{-0}-4B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{2} & =-3A-4B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{19}{2}=-3A-4B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $B=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $0.3$ seconds, in amperes, is\n\n$$\nI(0.3)=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-0.9}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{-1.2}≈0.444,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186547079 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e44f91621134e91a9f742811897940b87ffc24bea4b15100e43d2aa15a8c4330.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, th