92186576411 true] [17592186548063 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =