24} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le 0.625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.625).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.625-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.81\\le Z\\le 1.96) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.96)-P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.96)=0.9750,\\,Φ(-1.81)=0.0351.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.9750-0.0351 \\\\  & =0.9399.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186552404 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=224,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{56\\,000}{100\\,000}=0.56.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=224(0.56)=125.44>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=224(1-0.56)=98.56>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=224$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.56,\\frac{0.56(1-0.56)}{224}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,0.0011).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $112$ and $140$ of subscribers in the sample have a digital subscription, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{112}{224}\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{140}{224} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\widehat{\\,p