}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186555766 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =-\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)}^{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186555802 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le -2).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le -2) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{-2-(-7)}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1) & =Φ(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1)=0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le -2)=0.8413.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186555802 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le -2).