wing table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n\\left(-6,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),\\,(-5,0),\\,(3,1),\\,\\left(10,\\frac{3}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/de/ded8b752a5acdd94b4e73a4cf7b301ed0d616b0cfd35896b5c9e5275fc8ca2ad.png)" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186558522 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9} &= -(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9} &= (3x)^{2}-4x+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3x)+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9}) &= -{(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186558522 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9} &= -\\left(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9} &= (3x)^{2}-4x+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3x\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {\\left(3x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\