e $x=-{y}^{2}-\\frac{8y}{5},$ we just have to reflect the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ over the line $y=x,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/04/049683350600f401aa18feb6d5ea5662965ba0740f4b23a524e93e40f61f0bc4.png)" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186558564 1074 "To plot $x=-{y}^{2}-\\frac{8y}{5},$ we will first plot the function $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x.$\n\nThe curve $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ is a parabola. It has the following factored form:\n\n$$\ny=-x\\left(x+\\frac{8}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form above, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/26/26a35ba2e93c08af12f916f16ad40f46a8ea0242e23bc516d279a015094db872.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-\\frac{8y}{5},$ we just have to reflect the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ over the line $y=x,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/04/049683350600f401aa18feb6d5ea5662965ba0740f4b23a524e93e40f61f0bc4.png)" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186558573 1074 "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ into $x-y=1,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186558573 1074 "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ into $x-y=1,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186558845 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)+5=-2+5 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})+3=3+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(3,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,6)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)-(-2)) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)+2) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1+3 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$