ins *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the left of the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8f/8f182873a8384097b56152284e67189e36f0a79a0b2bbda8926a2f8279db7ddd.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,1)$ and $Q\\left(2,\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66c8cb59cceadd43e3c4316925f1dd624c2e77a6ec547c9f9a1a02846bb537fb.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality $y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186561049 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{7}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{28}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(28\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={a}_{7}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{7} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186561