aligned}f({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186562433 1074 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186562447 1074 "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(