ight)-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0\\right)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186563735 1074 "From the equation $y=(6x-1)(3x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{12}.$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 false] [17592186563735 1074 "From the equation $y=(6x-1)(3x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{6}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{12}.$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [17592186563738 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=\\left\\langle \\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5\\right\\rangle .$" 13194139545758 17592186576411 true] [1759218656